# R concepts Part 3
# R Concepts Tutorial - Part 3: Functions
# A function is a reusable command that performs a specific task.
# Functions usually have:
# 1. A function name
# 2. Parentheses ()
# 3. An input inside the parentheses, called an "argument"
# For example, the c() function combines several values into one vector (ordered collection of values):
sample_ids <- c("Sample_01", "Sample_02", "Sample_03")
# Type the variable name and run the line to view its contents:
sample_ids
# The length() function counts the number of values in an object:
length(sample_ids)
# If you are unsure what a certain function does, and what argument it takes, you can put a "?" before the function and run that to bring up the documentation
